# Transparency scales: scale_alpha

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_alpha()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_alpha.html), which covers
`scale_alpha_continuous()`, `scale_alpha_binned()`, `scale_alpha_discrete()` and
`scale_alpha_ordinal()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code
first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it
is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

An alpha scale maps a column to transparency, a way to fade the less important observations.
`scale_alpha()` is short for `scale_alpha_continuous()`, for a column of numbers;
`scale_alpha_binned()` cuts the numbers into bins first, and `scale_alpha_discrete()` and
`scale_alpha_ordinal()` are for discrete and ordered values. Giggly has none of them yet, nor the
`alpha` aesthetic they map.

## Mapping transparency

```r
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point(aes(alpha = year))

# The default range of 0.1-1.0 leaves all data visible
p
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `alpha` and the legend that reads it back.

## Invisible points

```r
# Include 0 in the range to make data invisible
p + scale_alpha(range = c(0, 1))
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `alpha` and the legend that reads it back, and `scale_alpha()` and its
`range` option.

## Titling the legend

```r
# Changing the title
p + scale_alpha("cylinders")
```

Not in Giggly yet: mapping `alpha` and the legend that reads it back, and `scale_alpha()`, whose
first argument titles the legend.
